  "text": "**Solution 2 has the correct final answer, 6.** I could not verify it with tools, so this rests on my recollection of the literature rather than a derivation. I recall a result stating that a(3,n) = 6 for n ≥ 18, which matches Solution 2.\n\n**Solution 1 (answer 7)** is not supported. Its lower-bound argument only reaches \"at least 7\" by vague iteration, and its upper-bound step asserts a construction with exactly 7 longest paths without giving one. The rotation argument it sketches does not force the count up to 7 in a way that is shown to be tight.\n\n**Solution 2 (answer 6)** gives the right value, but its justification is weak. The construction (two blocks at a cut vertex, each with three Hamiltonian paths from that vertex) is not checked, and the claim that the product gives 6 is not clearly correct. Its lower-bound claim is asserted rather than proved. So I credit it for the final answer, not for the reasoning.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
